{
  "description": "NFX-10 §1 STUN at the relays a client signals through, from NFX-04 §3's 'nfx.stun_port'. In 'relays', 'relay' is a relay URL as a client holds it (a lowercase host; an IP address in canonical form, an IPv6 one in brackets) and 'nip11' its NIP-11 document as parsed JSON, or null when the client could not fetch or parse one. 'stun' is the STUN server the client may ask for that relay, a stun: URL, or null for none. A relay names one when its document's 'nfx' object has a 'kinds' list holding the integer 25050 and a 'stun_port' that is an integer from 1024 to 65535: stun:<host of the relay URL>:<stun_port>. The URL's own port and path are the relay's and play no part. A host that is an IP address must be global unicast, as in stun-select.json; a host name is taken as it is, except localhost and names under .localhost, which name none (a native client also resolves a name and asks only if every address is global unicast, which no vector can show). In 'cases', 'own' are the client's own STUN URLs, 'relays' the relays it signals through, in its order, each with its document, and 'creator' the stun: URLs of the servers the creator's own beacon names. 'selected' is what the client asks, in order: its own, each once; then each relay's server, in the order of its relays; then the creator's that are global unicast, on a port of 1024 or above; each stun: URL once, at most 'max' in all. No traffic is ever sent to an address or name of a vector.",
